use you can't wash him out  of there  back in the 80s and 90s at least most of  your frozen broccoli was coming from  Central  Mexico the Baguio region and it led to  work with researchers down there where  they were setting out pheromone traps  for these mods and checking them three  times a week every single week from 1987  to 2005  so as entomologist we don't get datasets  like this very often and this led to  some questions about what is going on in  this system that would lead to this kind  of pattern what we showed is three kind  of distinct periods here where the  pattern was changing so one where there  was this kind of regular cyclical  pattern this is using time series  analysis and then a period where the  populations increased and the  fluctuations got really wild and then a  period where they drop down again but  the same pattern of how increases  follows decreases has been maintained  since this this drop-off and this had a  little bit more to do with weather than  anything else well what we were finding  is in looking at what was going on in  this agro-ecosystem that would it best  explain what was going on with this  particular pest it was really a lot  about the amount of broccoli that they  were growing so they went from 8,000  hectares to 40,000 hectares of broccoli  during this period that's a heck of a  lot of broccoli and what it does is  create an enormous carrying capacity for  pests like this but the pest wasn't the  only issue they were starting to have  problems with groundwater with  salinization of the soil with markets  with labor availability the extent that  the processing company down there that  were part of this industry we're already  looking at other parts of the world to  move and get out of this region of  central Mexico so if you keep focusing  on just one problem then usually you  miss what's going on in the system we  have a grand tradition in entomology of  solving one problem and creating three  more in the process and tha